/**
 * @mainpage mfemElasticity
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 * Extensions to the <a href="https://mfem.org">MFEM</a> library for
 * quasi-static elastic and viscoelastic problems in geophysics, including
 * self-gravitation. Serial and parallel (MPI) paths are provided throughout.
 *
 * @section contents Library contents
 *
 * @subsection sec_index Component ordering
 * `index.hpp` fixes the component ordering and the node/component indexing
 * of vector, matrix, symmetric and trace-free symmetric tensor fields
 * (SymmetricComponentOrder, VectorIndex, MatrixIndex, SymmetricMatrixIndex,
 * TraceFreeSymmetricMatrixIndex). Every integrator, interpolator and
 * coefficient below follows it.
 *
 * @subsection sec_integrators Integrators, interpolators and coefficients
 * `bilininteg.hpp` and `lininteg.hpp` hold mixed integrators between vector,
 * scalar and tensor nodal spaces (the non-standard terms of the
 * self-gravitating weak form and the internal-variable couplings of
 * viscoelasticity), the strain interpolators, the boundary integrators of
 * the fluid–solid interface terms, ElasticTensorIntegrator for general
 * anisotropic elasticity, and TransformedDiffusionIntegrator for problems
 * posed on a reference domain. `coefficient.hpp` holds general-purpose
 * coefficients, and `elastic_tensor.hpp` the elastic tensor coefficients
 * (isotropic, transversely isotropic, Voigt, rotated, deviatoric projection)
 * in the Mandel convention of SymmetricTensorBasis. The mathematical
 * formulation of each is in its class documentation.
 *
 * @subsection sec_poisson Exterior Poisson problems
 * `poisson.hpp` provides the matrix-free Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator on a
 * spherical outer boundary (PoissonDtNOperator) and the multipole
 * right-hand-side operators, all built on the real orthonormal harmonics of
 * `spherical_harmonics.hpp` (SurfaceHarmonics), which also synthesises
 * fields from coefficients and analyses finite-element fields on spherical
 * boundaries (BoundaryHarmonicCoefficients). `mesh.hpp` has the geometric
 * helpers.
 *
 * @subsection sec_submesh Coupling a mesh and its SubMesh
 * `submesh.hpp`: SubMeshDofInjection, the signed dof injection of a space on
 * a SubMesh into the corresponding space on the parent mesh, and
 * SubMeshMixedBilinearForm / ParSubMeshMixedBilinearForm, mixed forms whose
 * two spaces live on a mesh and one of its SubMeshes.
 *
 * @subsection sec_problems Quasi-static problems and rheologies
 * LinearQuasiStaticProblem is the interface of a linear quasi-static
 * equilibrium problem; LinearQuasiStaticProblemBase implements it on a
 * serial or parallel displacement space, with
 * LinearQuasiStaticTractionProblem and LinearQuasiStaticClampedProblem as
 * reference problems. The material is a Rheology: elastic or generalised
 * Maxwell, isotropic or anisotropic, optionally with state-dependent
 * relaxation times (RelaxationLaw), and different in different regions of
 * the body (CompositeRheology).
 *
 * @subsection sec_selfgrav Self-gravitation
 * LinearQuasiStaticSelfGravitatingProblem couples the displacement on a
 * SubMesh of the solid body to the potential perturbation on the enclosing
 * ball, with the DtN outer condition and optional fluid regions
 * (FluidRegion). It implements the same interface, so the viscoelastic layer
 * runs on it unchanged.
 *
 * @subsection sec_visco Viscoelasticity
 * ViscoelasticOperator evolves the internal variables of a generalised
 * Maxwell body around any LinearQuasiStaticProblem, with explicit, implicit
 * and exponential time stepping (ExponentialEulerSolver,
 * ExponentialTrapezoidSolver, AdaptiveExponentialTrapezoidSolver).
 *
 * @subsection sec_null_space Null-space projection
 * NullSpaceProjector holds an orthonormal basis of a (near-)null space, and
 * ProjectedSolver / ProjectedOperator run a Krylov method on the projected
 * system @f$P A P@f$ with the projected preconditioner @f$P M P@f$.
 * MakeRigidModeProjector() builds the projector for the rigid modes of a
 * displacement space; this is how problems with traction boundary conditions
 * are solved (see `examples/quasi_static_elasticity.cpp`).
 *
 * @section notes Method notes
 * The Markdown notes in the source tree's `doc/` directory record the
 * equations, the reasons for the main design choices and what has been
 * learned: `submesh_coupling.md`, `self_gravitation.md`,
 * `viscoelasticity.md`, `elastic_tensors.md`, `null_space.md` and
 * `mfem_notes.md`.
 *
 * @section install Installation
 * MFEM must be built first, serial or parallel. Configuration is with CMake;
 * in-source builds are refused. The options are:
 * - **`MFEM_DIR`**: the MFEM build or installation. Set it explicitly when
 *   several versions of MFEM are installed (serial and parallel, say).
 * - **`USE_MPI`** (default `OFF`): build against a parallel MFEM and enable
 *   the parallel classes, examples and tests.
 * - **`MPI_C_COMPILER`**, **`MPI_CXX_COMPILER`**: the MPI compiler wrappers
 *   MFEM was built with; needed when several MPI installations are present.
 * - **`BUILD_EXAMPLES`** (default `OFF`): build the programs in `examples/`.
 * - **`BUILD_TESTS`** (default `OFF`): build the test suite in `tests/`.
 * - **`BUILD_DOCS`** (default `OFF`): generate this documentation.
 * - **`GENERATE_MESHES`** (default: on with examples or tests): generate the
 *   gmsh meshes the examples and tests read, with the Python scripts in
 *   `meshes/`; `MESHES_PYTHON` names the interpreter to use.
 *
 * An example configuration of a parallel build:
 * ```bash
 * cmake -S mfemElasticity -B mfemElasticityBuild \
 *   -DUSE_MPI=ON \
 *   -DMFEM_DIR=[path to mfem build] \
 *   -DMPI_C_COMPILER=[path to mpi c compiler] \
 *   -DMPI_CXX_COMPILER=[path to mpi c++ compiler] \
 *   -DBUILD_EXAMPLES=ON \
 *   -DBUILD_TESTS=ON
 * ```
 * `INSTALL.md` has the details.
 *
 * @section proj_structure Project structure
 * - `include/`, `src/`: the library.
 * - `examples/`: example programs (`BUILD_EXAMPLES`).
 * - `tests/`: googletest suite and MPI test programs (`BUILD_TESTS`).
 * - `meshes/`: Python scripts generating the gmsh meshes into the build
 *   tree's `data/`.
 * - `data/`: MFEM-format meshes copied to the build tree.
 * - `doc/`: Doxygen configuration and the method notes.
 */
